  // read as long stripes sliding about; a real breeze roughens the pond every way at once. Each train is
  // weak (together ~0.025 rms slope, a calm pond), and falling rain flattens them further.
  // Steepness falls off toward the short end (slope ∝ λ^1.5): the short ripples are feeble, so the light
  // patterns on the bed come from the 10–25 cm waves, as in a real pond — large, curving cells.
  // Many trains at random lengths and headings: a few regular ones cross into a visible lattice.
  // A sheltered garden pond is nearly a mirror. Wind reaches it in gusts: patches of fine ripples
  // ("cat's paws") that drift across with the breeze and die away; outside them the water is still.
  // Inside a patch the ripples run every way (32 trains, 5–20 cm, none dominant — a few regular trains
